WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.890
Every week, someone forwards the same

2
00:00:02.890 --> 00:00:05.710
scam to our class group: “Free data for

3
00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:07.330
every student, click here!”

4
00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:09.160
What if we taught a computer

5
00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:10.580
to catch these for us?

6
00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:15.120
Today: how machines learn, in five

7
00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:16.560
steps you could follow yourself.

8
00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:20.130
Step one: collect examples.

9
00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:22.570
Your class gathers a hundred

10
00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:24.690
messages, takes out names and

11
00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:27.270
numbers, and keeps twenty aside for later.

12
00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:29.910
Step two: label them.

13
00:00:30.490 --> 00:00:33.670
Each message gets a tag: safe, or scam.

14
00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:36.870
Those labels are the right answers.

15
00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:39.850
Step three: train.

16
00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:42.040
The computer searches the

17
00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:43.740
labelled messages for patterns.

18
00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:46.760
Scams often say “free”,

19
00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:48.560
“click” or “urgent”.

20
00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:50.980
Safe ones talk about homework,

21
00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:52.840
matches and meetings.

22
00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:55.310
What it learns is kept as

23
00:00:55.310 --> 00:00:57.110
numbers, and that’s the model.

24
00:00:58.330 --> 00:00:59.910
Step four: test.

25
00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:02.890
Give the model the twenty messages you

26
00:01:02.890 --> 00:01:05.090
kept aside, which it has never seen.

27
00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:08.060
It guesses each one, and you

28
00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:09.260
compare with your labels.

29
00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:12.130
Seventeen out of twenty right?

30
00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:13.360
Not bad.

31
00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:14.960
Too many wrong?

32
00:01:15.710 --> 00:01:17.850
Collect more examples and train again.

33
00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:21.330
That’s machine learning: the computer

34
00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:23.670
finds patterns in labelled examples,

35
00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:26.330
instead of a person writing every rule.

36
00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:30.470
Collect, label, train, test.

37
00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:33.500
Then step five: use it to

38
00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:34.400
sort new messages.

39
00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:37.490
It’s like revising for exams.

40
00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:40.100
If you memorise last year’s

41
00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:42.260
paper, you’ll ace that paper.

42
00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:45.250
But the real exam has new questions.

43
00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:47.810
Only new questions show whether

44
00:01:47.810 --> 00:01:48.810
you learned the method.

45
00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:50.490
Quick check!

46
00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:53.990
Our model scored twenty out of twenty

47
00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:55.430
on the messages it trained on.

48
00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:57.000
Is it ready to use?

49
00:01:58.350 --> 00:01:58.910
Not yet.

50
00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:01.580
It may simply have memorised those

51
00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:04.280
messages, like memorising a past paper.

52
00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:07.420
Only messages it has never seen show

53
00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:08.440
whether it learned the patterns.

54
00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:12.020
Variety matters as much as numbers.

55
00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:14.920
Say every safe message you

56
00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:16.880
collected came from teachers.

57
00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:19.490
Then a friend’s message full of

58
00:02:19.490 --> 00:02:21.230
slang might look like a scam.

59
00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:25.010
And if every scam was in English, a scam

60
00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:27.250
in another language may slip through.

61
00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:30.170
More examples help, and more

62
00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:31.630
varied ones help most.

63
00:02:32.990 --> 00:02:34.910
In the lab, you teach the

64
00:02:34.910 --> 00:02:35.610
machine yourself.

65
00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:39.600
Two groups, safe message and phishing,

66
00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:41.620
start with two examples each.

67
00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:44.750
Add your own, then test a new message,

68
00:02:45.190 --> 00:02:47.130
like “Click now to keep your account”.

69
00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:49.760
It shows its guess, and

70
00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:51.200
how confident it is.

71
00:02:52.390 --> 00:02:52.930
Your turn.

72
00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:56.140
A scam filter learned only from

73
00:02:56.140 --> 00:02:57.900
messages promising free data.

74
00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:01.460
Then a fake scholarship offer arrives.

75
00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:03.170
What’s most likely?

76
00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:07.520
It might miss it: a model only knows the

77
00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:09.080
kinds of examples it was shown.

78
00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:12.140
Remember the five steps: collect,

79
00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:16.020
label, train, test, use.

80
00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:19.620
Always test on examples it has never seen.

81
00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:22.010
And more varied examples

82
00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:23.650
make a better model.

83
00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:27.730
Try it yourself: play “Be the machine”

84
00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:29.270
with a friend or someone at home.

85
00:03:29.850 --> 00:03:31.870
Draw shapes that follow a secret

86
00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:34.310
pattern, say, all with a dot

87
00:03:34.310 --> 00:03:36.590
inside, and shapes that don’t.

88
00:03:37.070 --> 00:03:38.450
Let them work out the pattern.

89
00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:40.960
Then test them with new shapes.
